<section class="content-section">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Converting GMT to PST</h2>
  <div class="use-cases">
    <article>
      <h3>1. Date Boundary Errors</h3>
      <p>
        When converting GMT hours between 12:00 AM and 7:59 AM, subtracting 8 hours steps back into the previous evening in PST. Failing to update the date header in calendar invites can lead to missed appointments.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>2. Misinterpreting PST as PT</h3>
      <p>
        PST specifically means UTC-8 (winter standard time). Using PST terminology during summer when California is actually on PDT (UTC-7) creates confusion during scheduling.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>3. Overlooking Spring/Fall DST Shift Weeks</h3>
      <p>
        Assuming the gap is strictly 8 hours during March and October transition weeks will lead to 1-hour scheduling mismatches between European and American calendars.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

